<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 11 and below (android:fullBackupContent). Belt-and-suspenders with
  android:allowBackup="false": exclude ALL app-managed data from any backup set.
  Spec §6 privacy invariant #1 — Android Auto Backup would otherwise upload the
  conversation DB, model/adapter files, and offline-auth cache to the user's
  Google Drive, silently violating "nothing a user types leaves the device".

  ⚠ `root` is the line that actually covers our data, and it was missing until
  Phase 3.2's design work went looking for somewhere to put credential blobs.
  Verified in AOSP's parser rather than from a doc example:

  - FullBackup.java `getDirectoryForCriteriaDomain`: "root" -> ROOT_DIR, where
    ROOT_DIR = ceContext.getDataDir(); "file" -> FILES_DIR = getFilesDir().
    Those are DIFFERENT directories — files/ is a child of the data dir.
  - Tauri resolves app_data_dir() to `activity.dataDir` on Android
    (tauri/src/path/android.rs -> PathPlugin.kt `getDataDir`, SDK_INT >= 24;
    our minSdk is 24, so the other branch is unreachable). So cloud-cache.json,
    auth-cache/, the conversation DB, models/ and resources/ all sit directly
    in the DATA DIR ROOT — the one domain these rules did not name.

  The four domains below were therefore excluding directories we do not use.
  Not a live leak — allowBackup="false" is decisive — but the second layer
  covered the empty set while reading as though it covered everything. Same
  family as `bundle.resources = {}`.

  Two more things checked in the parser, because a silently malformed rule is
  the exact failure this comment exists to prevent:

  - The `path` attribute is OPTIONAL. `extractCanonicalFile` substitutes "" for
    a null path, with the comment "Allow things like <include
    domain='sharedpref'/>", and `validateInnerTagContents` permits up to 2
    attributes on <exclude>. So the bare form below is well-formed by design,
    not merely tolerated.
  - Excluding a DIRECTORY prunes its whole subtree. BackupAgent.java's
    `fullBackupFileTree` matches excludes by exact canonical path and, on a
    match, `continue`s BEFORE enqueueing that directory's children — so the
    first entry of the root-domain walk is the data dir itself, and nothing
    beneath it is ever scanned.

  Effectiveness is only answerable on a device: see the `bmgr` item in
  docs/ops/release-config-audit.md, which asks whether the conversation DB is
  ABSENT FROM THE BACKUP SET — not whether this rule is present in this file.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="root" />
    <exclude domain="device_root" />
    <exclude domain="file" />
    <exclude domain="database" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
    <exclude domain="external" />
</full-backup-content>
